WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.820
May we now move on, please, to the next item?
This is item six: the NHS Performance Report.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.600
Barbara, I think you are going to outline
this for us.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:20.039
Barbara Hakin: Thank you, Chairman. This is
the third part of the three simple bits to

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:24.479
commissioning, because this is how we bring
to the Board the monitoring of how we are

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:32.040
doing. This is a new and simpler report for
the Board. It is simpler in terms of structure

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:36.100
and the way it is laid out.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:40.820
We look at two aspects. We look at NHS performance.
Furthermore, we always look at that first

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:45.329
because, in the final analysis, that is what
patients experience. That is what really matters

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:52.329
to us. We then look at the NHS England business
plan and how we are doing against some of

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.260
those commitments. The third part of this
report is the risks on the assurance framework.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:06.250
May I start with the NHS performance? Again,
I will not repeat what the Chief Executive

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:11.520
said[?] about our concerns about some of the
targets for making sure patients are seen

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.610
quickly. I will not talk about what we are
doing about urgent care in order to support

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.330
the A&amp;E standard. That standard gives us a
good indication of the pressure in the system

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.570
and whether patients are being seen in a timely
manner. I also will not talk about the 18

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.850
week treatment. However, all of those topics
are mentioned here.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:34.520
I think it will be worth pointing out to the
Board that we are concerned at the moment

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:39.590
about diagnostics and the patients who have
waited longer than six weeks. It has always

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:43.860
seemed to me, particularly in the years when
I had much more involvement with patients

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.940
as a GP, that actually the thing which concerned
them the most was not having a diagnosis.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.910
When you have a diagnosis, particularly if
the diagnosis is not a life threatening disorder,

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:57.900
then waiting to be treated is not quite as
difficult as actually waiting to find out

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.990
what is the matter with you. I think that
is the worst thing to have to do.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:08.330
The times that patients are waiting for diagnosis
is just beginning to slip. We are therefore

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:13.360
doing a lot of work with TDA to monitor our
frontline organisations on why that is happening.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.150
We are particularly monitoring the cancer
targets. Whilst most of those are fine, our

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:26.150
worry is particularly about the 62 day wait.
That is a really important target. Again,

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:32.420
we need to get this back on line. The System
Resilience Groups have additional money. However,

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:38.700
we need to make sure that we find a way through
that and through their work to improve their

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:44.090
referral to treatment. There are the 18 weeks
and the A&amp;E but also, particularly, cancer

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:44.390
and diagnostics.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:51.390
On the next couple of pages, the report goes
on to talk about our major mandate commitments.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:56.630
Again, these are things which are really important
to patients, like being diagnosed for dementia

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:02.640
and what is happening in Winterbourne View.
Other national directors lead on the central

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.800
and policy parts of this programme. However,
I would like to assure the Board that we are

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.959
working through the regional and area teams.
Every single one of these gets our attention

00:03:11.959 --> 00:03:16.760
in the commissioning operations directorate
in our regular meetings. Both regional and

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.819
area directors, working with their teams,
are working very hard to make sure all these

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:24.660
important things are delivered for patients.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:30.770
That was the first thing on the NHS. May I
draw the Board's attention to our business

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:35.459
plan? You will see from the paper that three
of the areas in our business plan where we

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:40.360
have concerns have very significant overlap:
elective care, urgent care and the challenged

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:44.849
geographies. Challenged geographies represents
the work we are doing with those geographies

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:50.670
we have identified as having the biggest difficulty
in delivering high quality, timely services

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.220
for patients.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:57.310
The other two are worries from our central
work, they are not about what is happening

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:04.310
in the NHS. One is the work plan on parity
of esteem. That work plan is being refreshed

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:10.160
and governance arrangements are being put
in place. Another worry is medical revalidation.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.629
Again, I do not know if other national directors
want to contribute on that.

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:19.329
However, I will just finish by identifying
that there are two risks in the Board assurance

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:26.329
framework which we have decided have reduced
in order. We have decided they do not necessarily

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:37.010
need to be brought at corporate level. The
first one is complaints. As you will remember,

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:40.830
this is a really essential service for patients
to ensure their complaints are addressed.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:46.120
We had a big problem early on in NHS England
with the numbers and the way we delivered

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:52.530
the service. However, we have managed to address
some of those. We think this is still an issue

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:57.230
for us; it is something we are being very
vigilant about. However, it is not necessarily

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:00.240
significant enough to be reported at this
level.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.720
The performance of 111 is a similar case.
We have had a slight dip recently, but it

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:11.780
is only a slight dip compared to the issues
we had a year or so ago. The performance issues

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:17.250
are now largely managed. We feel this is part
of the urgent care system. Therefore, it should

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:22.710
be reported as one of the areas we need to
get right in order to make the whole of the

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:29.710
urgent care, access to that urgent care and
hospitals meeting their targets what they

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:31.169
should be.

00:05:31.169 --> 00:05:38.000
Three risks are going on to the BAF: genomics,
the Better Care Fund and referral to treatment

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.290
time. We have talked about the Better Care
Fund already, so I will not go on that. However,

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:47.630
referral to treatment time is going on for
a couple of reasons. One is certainly because

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:52.850
we have seen it deteriorate. However, secondly,
in a way we had almost squeezed referral to

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:58.139
treatment time into the emergency care risk.
It had been that emergency care rises and

00:05:58.139 --> 00:06:03.400
it has a knock on effect. Of course, that
is true. It does. However, we felt that referral

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.650
to treatment was important and significant
enough for patients that it should be a separate

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:13.610
risk. Therefore, the BAF shows those changes
to the register. Obviously, on the BAF summary

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:19.210
on page 5 you can see that the majority of
BAF risks have not moved. There are a couple

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:25.710
of movements. However, one is in 111, which
we are saying is coming off the register anyway.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:29.979
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Barbara. Any comments
or observations? Margaret?

00:06:29.979 --> 00:06:36.270
Margaret Casely Hayford: Thank you, Barbara.
It is very simple. It is very clear. I do

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:42.960
have one point in connect with NHS 111. If
we are looking at the performance against

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:49.960
service level, there is still quite a gap
against the target of responses within the

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:57.130
targeted period. I think that targeted period
is 60 seconds. I think we are quite significantly

00:07:00.259 --> 00:07:06.620
below target. You have noted that as amber.
However, in my view it is amber to red if

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:09.160
there is that sort of gap.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:16.160
Barbara Hakin: I accept that. Obviously we
want to make sure that every call is answered

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:24.270
in that time. However, compared to the problems
we had when this service was initiated, the

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:29.330
vast majority of patients still get their
calls answered within that time. However,

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:33.120
we do need to make it better. And it is one
or two providers, whereas, again, last year

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.500
we were seeing problems across the Board,
with the whole service. However, we are working

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:43.410
very, very closely with those providers, or
rather our CCG colleagues are, to make sure

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:48.320
we get that target back on line. That is particularly
the case for the abandonment rate. That is

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:53.500
really important because that suggests that
patients are giving up. There is always some

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:57.930
abandonment. That will never be zero for a
variety of reasons: patients themselves change

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:02.539
their minds. However, we need to watch that
really importantly because that is absolutely

00:08:02.539 --> 00:08:04.720
an outcome for me rather than a process measure.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:11.720
Malcolm Grant: Good. Right, thank you. Thank
you, Barbara, for the new model of report.

00:08:12.199 --> 00:08:14.479
I think it is appreciated by the Board.

00:08:14.479 --> 00:08:20.889
May we move on, then, to paper 7: Primary
Care Co Commissioning. I am going to invite

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:26.280
Simon to say a few words about co commissioning
and the background to the paper.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:33.280
Thank you, Malcolm. Two months ago we put
on the table a new option for CCGs who wanted

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:40.490
to come forward and propose ways in which
they could get more control over the ways

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:46.040
primary medical services in their areas were
funded and designed. Obviously, the context

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:52.890
for this is the changing needs of the people
we are serving, including the increasing numbers

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:59.790
of older people with[?] frailty and multiple
long term conditions. Given that context we

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:05.910
need much greater integration. We need to
overcome the funding silos that have historically

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:12.910
existed between GP services, community nursing
services, hospital outpatient services, ambulance

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:20.010
services and social services. Therefore, as
a first step in this direction, we have said

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:26.709
we are willing to work with CCGs who want
to either do this on our behalf or do this

00:09:26.709 --> 00:09:28.279
with us.

00:09:28.279 --> 00:09:32.529
We have been very pleased by the enthusiasm
that this opportunity, which NHS England has

00:09:32.529 --> 00:09:39.529
offered, has been greeted with. As the paper
sets out, nine in ten CCGs across the country

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:47.040
have come forward with a variety of proposals
as to how they might work with us on this.

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:52.050
We propose taking a permissive approach. It
does not have to be the same everywhere. However,

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:59.050
there obviously needs to be very strong governance
oversight to ensure that there is a clear

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:10.709
mechanism that committee of this Board can
be confident in to ensure conflicts of interest

00:10:10.709 --> 00:10:11.260
are dealt with. That is particularly the case
for the third of the options with the greatest

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:14.470
level of CCG control. However, we cannot let
that issue stand in the way of the broader

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.029
service changes which we know patients need.

00:10:17.029 --> 00:10:23.640
I am going to ask Barbara to run through some
of the detail of how we propose assessing

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:28.450
those individual applications. She will also
give details of the assurance and approval

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:34.940
process that we would embark on. However,
I think the headline here is that, frankly,

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:40.480
we are pushing at an open door. Or rather,
CCGs are pushing at an open door: our open

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.240
door. What they want and what we want is one
and the same.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:45.580
Malcolm Grant: Barbara?

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:52.580
Barbara Hakin: Thank you. I would just like
to expand on the benefits for patients of

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:58.709
co commissioning and CCGs having a much greater
say in primary medical care services. The

00:10:58.709 --> 00:11:02.470
paper says primary care. It should say primary
medical care. However, it does explain in

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:09.470
the paper that we cannot delegate the other
aspects. I will explain. Not district nurses,

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:19.220
but doctors, GPs, dentists, opticians and
pharmacists are called independent contractors.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:26.220
They are all primary care contractors. We
tend, sometimes, to just use the term primary

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:31.839
care when we mean the GP bit. Actually we
need to be very clear that primary care does

00:11:31.839 --> 00:11:35.470
include all those other vital services as
well.

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:42.470
However, we do know that GP services are so
local and it is so important they serve the

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:52.730
needs of patients within their communities.
Often, general practices have many, many years

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:57.990
of relationship with the community. It is
a very stable part of the NHS. It is a very

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:04.990
personal relationship for patients. There
are obvious reasons why those services are

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:13.000
commissioned by NHS England. It was to allow
CCGs to become mature, governing bodies to

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.300
bed and to accommodate worries about conflicts
of interest. However, it has always seemed

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:19.190
that it was inappropriate that the commissioning
of those services should be by NHS England.

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:22.660
Despite the sterling efforts of its area teams,
NHS England does not have anything like the

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:25.779
same local presence as our CCGs.

00:12:25.779 --> 00:12:31.100
Of course, our CCGs are membership organisations
of general practice. Hence, there is an issue

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:36.399
about conflicts of interest. However, we should
not underestimate the intimate relationship

00:12:36.399 --> 00:12:41.570
with their patient that gives them. Many of
them have lively patient participation groups.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:47.010
They can start to understand what their patients
need. The CCGs have a very local presence

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:51.910
with stakeholders, local authority and social
care. That can all be brought to bear in designing

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.680
the services.

00:12:53.680 --> 00:13:00.680
The other reason is continuity. Patients just
have a set of care needs. At the moment we

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:08.130
have a situation whereby that absolutely vital
first contact starts and ends there. That

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:08.450
maybe the case even though the patients then
have services within the community and hospitals.

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:13.750
We should not underestimate how much patient
contact sits within the whole of primary care

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:20.750
contracting, including optometrists, dentists
and pharmacists as well as general practice.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:32.740
We have a scenario where one group of people
is commissioning that primary care, yet another

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:37.110
group of people is commissioning the community
services and another group of people is commissioning

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:38.810
the hospital services.

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:44.769
I will give an example of a patient with a
long term condition, perhaps somebody who

00:13:44.769 --> 00:13:51.769
has heart failure or somebody who has a chronic
lung disorder. That patient will have a very

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:54.920
close relationship with their general practitioner.
However, they will also often have a close

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:58.350
relationship with a lot of other staff in
the general practice. A lot of practices have

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:02.730
a practice nurse. They might have a specialist
heart failure nurse. They might be employed

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:06.649
by the practice or they might be employed
by the community nurses. Those patients will

00:14:06.649 --> 00:14:11.709
also have a very intimate relationship with
the community nursing staff. They are commissioned

00:14:11.709 --> 00:14:17.120
by the CCG. Many of them will go to the hospital
for services. That is commissioned via the

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:22.290
GP. Some of them may have specialist needs,
which we will commission. The most important

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:27.740
specialised services are a smaller subset.
However, the vast majority of patients who

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:33.100
are frail, elderly or have any sort of long
term condition need a joined up approach across

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:34.940
all that care.

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:38.690
Therefore this is designed to make sure that,
either through joint commissioning or through

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:44.370
the delegation of budgets, we can start to
see that focus on that patient who needs services

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:51.100
from the GP, out of hours, community services
and a hospital. We can make sure those services

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:51.720
are seamless.

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:56.810
This paper, first of all, identifies some
of the things we expect to see delivered as

00:14:56.810 --> 00:15:03.440
benefits. I have just explained some of those.
However, I think others include closer working

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:09.480
with social care because of that local nature.
It also includes raising the standards of

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:15.000
quality. CCGs do have a responsibility to
improve the quality of primary care. However,

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.970
they will that, to some extent, they do not
have some of the levers because they do not

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:23.730
commission. Additionally, we should not underestimate
how much quality is risen by peer pressure

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:30.730
and people working together across groups
of practices. However, there is also understanding

00:15:31.399 --> 00:15:36.600
of local needs and very specific local communities.
A lot of our CCGs say they could do more to

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:43.329
tackle inequalities if they had that focus,
because of their local need and understanding.

00:15:43.329 --> 00:15:50.329
Encouragingly, as Simon said, we had very
significant numbers of expressions of interest.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:57.600
When we identifying how we would support CCGs,
we determined that the expressions of interest

00:16:01.079 --> 00:16:04.560
were broadly falling into three categories.
In the first category the CCGs just said,

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:08.899
'We would really like more influence. We would
like to be able to work more closely with

00:16:08.899 --> 00:16:15.899
the area teams.' Only 15 CCGs do not want
to do co commissioning; 196 do. Of the 196

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:24.230
that want to do co commissioning there are
only 19 in that category of influence. The

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:31.230
biggest category is 110. 110 CCGs want to
do joint commissioning. That means they would

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:35.260
have a joint committee with our area team
and they would all work together on the commissioning

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:42.260
of primary care. Finally, 74 want to take
delegated authority for some aspects of primary

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:44.589
medical care commissioning.

00:16:44.589 --> 00:16:50.300
There are two things that we need to work
on over the coming months. There is a lot

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:57.300
of work to do to ensure what we do is the
right thing to do. It is permissive. However,

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:04.139
it does protect the patients, in terms of
the quality of services. It also protects

00:17:04.139 --> 00:17:06.149
the taxpayer vis-a-vis any conflicts of interest.

00:17:06.149 --> 00:17:10.509
We need to get all the work done around the
joint committees. We need to identify exactly

00:17:10.509 --> 00:17:17.509
how they will work. That will be coming through
the system. The CCG oversight committee, the

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:24.419
assurance committee, will be ensuring that
those processes are right. We will producing

00:17:24.419 --> 00:17:28.690
templates, because we do not want the wheel
to be reinvented many times across the country

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:30.789
on the joint committees.

00:17:30.789 --> 00:17:37.789
We will be looking particularly at those expressions
of interest which include delegated authority.

00:17:39.109 --> 00:17:43.529
As the Chief Executive rightly pointed out,
those are the ones where we have to be very

00:17:43.529 --> 00:17:49.739
vigilant about conflicts of interest. However,
I think times have changed enormously over

00:17:49.739 --> 00:17:56.080
the last year or so. CCGs which were immature
organisations are now robust statutory organisations.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:59.729
They have their governing bodies. They have
a lot of patient/public participation. Furthermore,

00:17:59.729 --> 00:18:05.580
they have a lot of members on the governing
bodies who are not GPs. However, we will need

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:09.950
to test every single one of those. We need
to make sure that, where they are making decisions

00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:13.379
about GP services, those conflicts of interest
are covered.

00:18:13.379 --> 00:18:18.850
In the early days of CCGs we produced guidance
that was very clear about what they had to

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:25.019
do. They could have had issues of conflict
of interest before co commissioning. Therefore,

00:18:25.019 --> 00:18:31.239
they should already have arrangements in place
whereby GPs who have any interest declare

00:18:31.239 --> 00:18:37.489
it, as we do here and stand aside from any
discussions or decisions that are to be made.

00:18:37.489 --> 00:18:43.820
They have arrangements for adequate patient
and public voice through lay members. They

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:50.029
are not called non executives but they are
similar to non executive members. Where necessary,

00:18:50.029 --> 00:18:54.159
decisions can be made where the majority of
people making the decisions are not the GPs

00:18:54.159 --> 00:18:56.450
if they all have an interest. There may be
occasions where too many of the members of

00:18:56.450 --> 00:19:02.309
the governing body are conflicted. On those
occasions the CCG can actually defer the decision

00:19:02.309 --> 00:19:09.309
to another CCG. They can also check and get
double assurance from our area team that the

00:19:11.729 --> 00:19:16.960
decision they have made is appropriate. We
are encouraging them to use the right contracting

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:20.999
models. Specifically, we are making sure they
use open and competitive tendering for some

00:19:20.999 --> 00:19:24.679
of these services, where that would be appropriate.
Those are the sort of oversight arrangements

00:19:24.679 --> 00:19:25.580
we will be putting in place.

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:29.399
There is, therefore, lots of work to do as
we go through the next few months. We have

00:19:29.399 --> 00:19:36.399
our star area teams to give us an assessment
of which categories we think these expressions

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:45.190
fall into. Which ones are ready now? Which
ones do they think will be ready quite soon?

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:52.159
Which ones will be ready later? Encouragingly,
the vast majority are in the ready now or

00:19:52.159 --> 00:19:59.159
ready soon. There are 74 in the delegated
authority, if you will remember. Very specifically,

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:04.950
the area teams' early assessment is that about
20 of those would be ready now, 45 would be

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:11.549
ready soon and only nine would be ready later.
If you are working on the assumption that

00:20:11.549 --> 00:20:16.099
is the category in which we would have expected
the greatest delay, it just shows the preponderance

00:20:16.099 --> 00:20:21.099
that these plans coming forward are realistic.
They have been assessed as the ones that we

00:20:21.099 --> 00:20:24.509
would be in a position to support.

00:20:24.509 --> 00:20:31.509
Malcolm Grant: Barbara, thank you. Thank you
for that picture of the energy that is coming

00:20:31.599 --> 00:20:37.229
from the CCGs and those who are interested
in category C. Let me invite comments from

00:20:37.229 --> 00:20:39.349
colleagues. Ciaran?

00:20:39.349 --> 00:20:46.349
Ciaran Devane: There was a small comment you
made around the potential for this to move

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:55.970
workforce and have roles which combined some
of the specialist commissioning with what

00:20:58.799 --> 00:21:05.799
CCGs in primary care. I think that is probably
much more important than we really realise.

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:14.210
I think it was good to hear. I suppose that
is as much as I want to say.

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:23.210
Simon Stevens: Ciaran, the direction of travel
is clearly that we want to have, to the greatest

00:21:23.669 --> 00:21:29.279
possible extent, unified, population based
budgets that include primary care, local hospital

00:21:29.279 --> 00:21:33.190
and community services and specialised commissioning
services. Furthermore, CCGs are now up and

00:21:33.190 --> 00:21:36.029
running and firing on all cylinders. Therefore,
as we have been doing the review of the scope

00:21:36.029 --> 00:21:43.029
of specialised commissioning, we have been
looking at whether there is an opportunity

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:47.159
for CCGs to take more control and give more
direction to some of what is in the specialised

00:21:47.159 --> 00:21:50.599
commissioning pot.

00:21:50.599 --> 00:21:57.599
I think we will be coming back to you in September
or October with proposals for greater devolution

00:21:58.649 --> 00:22:03.899
of parts of the specialist commissioning responsibilities
as well. They will either go to CCGs individually

00:22:03.899 --> 00:22:08.859
or collectively. We will be thinking about
how that, in turn, maps onto an allocation

00:22:08.859 --> 00:22:09.149
process.

00:22:09.149 --> 00:22:13.559
Ciaran Devane: And it links into social care,
Better Care Fund and whatever as well.

00:22:13.559 --> 00:22:20.559
Simon Stevens: It does. As you know, one of
the things I am going to be doing next week

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:27.739
is setting out a new option for commissioning
jointly with health and social care at the

00:22:27.739 --> 00:22:29.450
LGA conference.

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:34.580
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Thank you. Moira?

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:41.580
Moira Gibb: Thank you, Malcolm. Barbara, it
seemed a very clear presentation, really,

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:43.989
about the potential benefits and risks. On
a detail point, will the committees in common

00:22:43.989 --> 00:22:45.940
meet in public?

00:22:45.940 --> 00:22:52.940
Barbara Hakin: That is a really good question.
I am not sure I know the answer. Rosa, have

00:22:52.979 --> 00:22:56.049
we done anything on that or is that something
we need to look at?

00:22:56.049 --> 00:23:01.549
Rosamond Roughton: No. I think it is something
we need to look at. We know that CCGs are

00:23:01.549 --> 00:23:06.419
holding many of their meetings in public.
Therefore, I think it does give a really good

00:23:06.419 --> 00:23:11.629
opportunity to have some of these local decisions
about primary care being made with public

00:23:11.629 --> 00:23:12.349
voice there.

00:23:12.349 --> 00:23:17.899
Barbara Hakin: Most certainly. If it were
as a joint committee, it would, like all our

00:23:17.899 --> 00:23:23.549
joint committees, need to report its actions
and give an overview to the CCG governing

00:23:23.549 --> 00:23:27.919
body. I kind of guess it would depend on the
depth detail that committee would do. However,

00:23:27.919 --> 00:23:32.789
patients have said to us, 'Where is the local
voice about the commissioning NHS England

00:23:32.789 --> 00:23:37.109
does?' If they meet in common it will mean
that, the same was as we would do here, locally

00:23:37.109 --> 00:23:42.149
the CCG governing bodies, all of which do
meet in public, would need to report what

00:23:42.149 --> 00:23:46.369
their committees had been talking about and
decided.

00:23:46.369 --> 00:23:52.830
Malcolm Grant: Good. Thank you. Good progress.
With population based commissioning on a local

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:59.830
basis with delegation from us, appropriate
oversight to assure against any inference

00:23:59.869 --> 00:24:04.549
of conflict of interest is going to be critically
important. That then stabilises the system.

00:24:04.549 --> 00:24:08.769
Therefore, we look to the executive team to
continue to work on this. Thank you for the

00:24:08.769 --> 00:24:09.019
report.

